WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.190
Favorite mistake?

2
00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.960
So when I was in first grade,

3
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.370
the pinnacle of my acting experience was when I was

4
00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:11.653
The Wicked Witch of the West in our first grade play.

5
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.060
In hindsight, I realize I only got that part

6
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.300
'cause I could read, not 'cause of my acting abilities.

7
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.530
And I convinced my mom to let me dye my hair green,

8
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:26.510
and she wanted me to do the spray-on dye,

9
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.220
and I convinced her to do the wash dye,

10
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.860
and it didn't come out for weeks.

11
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.023
And so in my first grade photos,

12
00:00:40.527 --> 00:00:45.527
it's tinged green and it's not terribly attractive,

13
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.370 line:15%
and I look really pretty self conscious,

14
00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.340 line:15%
'cause I know that my hair is still green.

15
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.173 line:15%
Oh!

16
00:00:52.173 --> 00:00:53.330 line:15%
And maybe the moral is,

17
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:54.930
I should have listened to my mother,

18
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.171
which is often the moral

19
00:00:56.171 --> 00:00:57.692
of this story.
(Drew laughing)

20
00:00:57.692 --> 00:01:00.030
(audience applauding)

21
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.080
Who is your favorite rebel right now?

22
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.610
'Cause your books are all about these women who-

23
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.410
Maybe Jane Goodall, who I do write about in the book,

24
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:12.380
just because she is still out there

25
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.480
fighting for conservation and fighting to put communities

26
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.280
at the center of their conservation efforts.

27
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.747
And at any point in time, that woman could have said,

28
00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.530
"I've done enough."

29
00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.290
What would it take for you to ever run for president?

30
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.700
Oh wow, that's not where I thought

31
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.170
that question was going.

32
00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.900
(audience applauding)

33
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.743
Oh gosh.

34
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.090
I just don't see that in my path right now, and yet,

35
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.120
I do think it's important that for any of us who care

36
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.120
about our country, who care about our world,

37
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.000
to constantly be asking ourselves,

38
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.940
should we run for public office?

39
00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.860
Because there are tens of thousands

40
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.560
of elected public offices in our country,

41
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.760
and it matters who's sitting in the White House,

42
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.120
as we have certainly seen recently.

43
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.290
It also matters who's sitting in city council,

44
00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.100
on a school board,

45
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.690
who's running the local sanitation commission.

46
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.060
All of these things really matter.

47
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:14.060
And so for me, writing, advocating, my podcast,

48
00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.540
using my platform in ways,

49
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.220
especially during Women's History Month

50
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.010
to lift up and celebrate women

51
00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.700
is what feels right and good for me.

52
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.550
But I don't know if that's always gonna be the answer.

53
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.550
But right now I feel well-represented,

54
00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.641
and well purposed in other ways.

55
00:02:31.641 --> 00:02:32.920
(audience applauding)

56
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.710
Well, I don't know anyone who has as much grace as you do.

57
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.280
I mean, you have really just lived a life,

58
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.050
and been a very admirable person the whole time.

59
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.890
And I really respect that.

60
00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.360
Well, I try.

61
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.123
I don't always get it right, but I always try.

62
00:02:55.264 --> 00:02:56.779
Persist.
Persist.

63
00:02:56.779 --> 00:02:58.800
(audience applauding)

64
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.110
And you can hear more from Chelsea on her amazing podcast

65
00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.430
called "In Fact With Chelsea Clinton."

66
00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.657
And you can pick up the latest in her glorious series,

67
00:03:08.657 --> 00:03:12.330
"She Persisted in Science," which is in stores now,

68
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.000
and it's phenomenal, such as you.

69
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.180
Thank you.
Thank you, Chelsea.

70
00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.435 line:15%
We'll be right back.
(audience applauding)

71
00:03:17.435 --> 00:03:20.018 line:15%
